.env
blib/
_build/
.build/
Build
Build.bat
MYMETA.json
MYMETA.yml
Makefile
MANIFEST
pm_to_blib
Developer-Dashboard-*.tar.gz
Developer-Dashboard-*/
.developer-dashboard/

# Operational logs. Untracked is not enough: the automation rounds' park stages
# and then resets untracked files, which deleted this directory and silently
# disabled the hourly FT99 sweep for its whole life (DD-493).
logs/
cover_db/
.perl5/
local/
audit-local/
Developer-Dashboard-0*
*.md
!SECURITY_CHECKS.md
!SKILLS.md
# DD-683: one glob instead of one negation per vault page. docs/ (plural) is
# the flat operator vault with no subdirectories, and every new page used to
# add its own !docs/<page>.md line at this same spot - a manufactured merge
# conflict between any two cards adding a page at once, whose only correct
# resolution was always "keep both". Widening this does NOT widen what
# ships: docs/ (plural) never reaches the release tarball regardless of git
# tracking (verified empirically - dzil build then grep the MANIFEST/tar for
# docs/, both zero, both before and after this change). The REASON is
# dist.ini's `exclude_match = ^docs/`, NOT anything about what GatherDir
# looks at: GatherDir declares no root here, so it gathers the whole
# distribution root and docs/ is excluded explicitly. DELETE THAT LINE AND
# docs/ SHIPS - it is the only protection, and .gitignore cannot stand in for
# it because dzil gathers from disk rather than from git (DD-401). See
# docs/docs-vault-vs-doc-directory.md. doc/ (singular, the shipped
# directory) keeps its own explicit per-file whitelist below, unchanged.
!docs/*.md
!doc/owasp-compliance-sow.md
!doc/security.md
!doc/update-and-release.md
!doc/integration-test-plan.md
!doc/testing.md
!doc/windows-testing.md
!integration/browser/
!integration/browser/run-bookmark-browser-smoke.pl
updates/

# The coverage gate takes an advisory lock so two gates cannot delete each
# other's database mid-run (DD-526). The lock is named after the database it
# guards and sits beside it, so the default cover_db at the root gives
# .cover_db.lock here, and a --database elsewhere locks beside itself.
.cover_db.lock
.*.lock

# Gate transcripts, written per run by whoever launched one.
.gate-logs/
.gate-*.log

# Per-ticket worktree sandboxes. Every ticket is worked in its own worktree cut
# from origin/master, named for the ticket reference, and merged back at the git
# gate. Those sandboxes hold whole checkouts and must never reach master, which
# is what ignoring them guarantees. Leaving them merely untracked relied on
# nobody ever running `git add -A` from the repo root - a rule that protects
# nothing the moment somebody, or something, forgets it.
.worktrees/

# Build spew from the dogfood runs, same class: untracked was never protection.
dogfood-output/
__pycache__/

# Gate artifacts: run-suite/coverage-run/gate-status write per-checkout state
# here (DD-652). Runtime output, never committed. Redundant with the blanket
# .claude/ rule below (DD-644), kept because it names the specific reason this
# one subdirectory is gitignored, which the blanket rule alone would not say.
.claude/state/

# Operator tooling is never part of the repo (owner, 2026-08-29):
# "no claude tool allows to be part of the repo. add to gitignore"
.claude/
